WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.400
Narrator: This is an early test video

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.875
for one of the White Spikes

00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:07.658
from 2021's "The Tomorrow War."

00:00:07.658 --> 00:00:10.250
But it's very different
from what the viewer sees

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.653
in the finished movie.

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:15.260
The creatures are not really conducive

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.800
to having an actor inside.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.808
Narrator: To get here,
Spectral Motion built

00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:22.840
a moving animatronic powered
by motors known as servos,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.870
which was used as a reference
for the visual effects team

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.170
and even ended up in
some of the final shots.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.020
Movement is what really sells

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.170
any animatronic character in a movie,

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.550
so roboticists like Mark
Setrakian work tirelessly

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.250
to nail down realistic motions,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.130
like these gigantic troll hands

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.090
from 2013's "Hansel &amp;
Gretel: Witch Hunters,"

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.860
which needed to move nimbly enough

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.510
to convincingly pick up
objects and throw punches.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.510
The performer who played Edward the troll

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.610
wore two animatronic hands,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.426
but they were so large
that the performer's hands

00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:58.730
only reached the troll's wrists.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.150
As he moved the hands around,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.910
the subtle finger motions were created

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.190
with radio-controlled hobby servos,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.020
like you'd find in a model plane.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.830
Each hand had 10 servos
spread throughout the fingers.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.280
Mark: Each finger actually
has two degrees of freedom.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.140
That means that there's a
base knuckle, like this,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.428
and then there's these

00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:18.400
two phalanges here that can also move.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.860
So you can map each
axis of these joysticks

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.110
to specific joints.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.580
In this case, you're mapping
them to the finger joints.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.390
So depending on how I map those,

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.840
you can optimize the hand
for doing different actions.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.160
Narrator: Toggling one joystick

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.160
created a gesture or a point.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.651
Running controls simultaneously

00:01:37.651 --> 00:01:39.026
for the thumb and index fingers,

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:41.020
as seen in this early test video,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.010
created a pinching motion used
to lift up the small object.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.350
The work done on top of the
servos is equally important.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.690
No matter how sophisticated
the radio controls,

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.281
the movements would feel off

00:01:52.281 --> 00:01:54.597
if each muscle and joint
that made up a hand

00:01:54.597 --> 00:01:57.400
wasn't in exactly the right place.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.810
One motion Mark observed in his own hands:

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.870
compression in this area.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.200
The upper palm had to line up
perfectly with the knuckles.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.510
To achieve this, Mark worked

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.936
with Spectral Motion's
sculptors from the start

00:02:08.936 --> 00:02:12.290
to make sure their model went
hand in hand with his servos.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.400
Mark: The mechanical design
starts with the sculpture.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.658
We can see these big wrinkly knuckles.

00:02:17.658 --> 00:02:18.830
Let's put a little tension on there,

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.532
like it would be in real life.

00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:24.006
So, that joint appears to be right here,

00:02:24.006 --> 00:02:26.364
and anatomically,

00:02:26.364 --> 00:02:29.920
that puts it right behind
this big fat pad right here.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.960
So the anatomy really has
to be based on reality,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.690
and that's where the best
mechanism designs start from.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:36.965
Narrator: The ways in which the mechanics

00:02:36.965 --> 00:02:39.810
and the outer layers work
together are even more important

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.650
when it comes to conveying
emotions in the face.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.040
That's where soft mechanics come in.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.530
These are cables embedded

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.730
in the outer layer of animatronics.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.913
Soft mechanics are often
used for animatronics

00:02:50.913 --> 00:02:53.310
that don't have traditional
bones and joints,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.280
like the petals on the Demogorgon's head

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.600
that Mark operated for "Stranger Things"

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.600
or Edward the troll's tongue.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.970
And in 2006's "Lady in the
Water," Mark and his team

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.840
wanted to give the wolflike
Scrunt an angry snarl.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.890
Here are the Scrunt's hard mechanics.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.200
This piece here both pulls the nose back

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.830
and lifts it up at the same time.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.400
But those hard mechanics
had to work hand in hand

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.020
with the softer elements
of the wolf's face,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.800
like these lips that pull
up and expose the fangs.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.720
And it looks a little odd
here without the skin on,

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.830
but when the skin's on,
it looks very realistic.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.490
Narrator: The skin itself
posed a problem, however.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.640
Unlike a real wolf, the Scrunt
had a coat made of grass,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.140
so one of the most
expressive parts of the face,

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.830
the eyebrows, weren't coming
through that thick layer.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.570
They're like these two
points, and they move around,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.240
and they have whiskers
sticking out of them.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.570
Narrator: So Mark actually
strayed from reality

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.210
and based its eyebrows on human eyebrows.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.280
Mark: It was necessary to
punch up that expression,

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.010
that there's no ambiguity about it,

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.800
when this thing is
snarling and it's angry,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.840
that those brows are selling
that the creature is angry.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.880
Narrator: When moving
down to the Scrunt's tail,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.223
Mark encountered a very tricky
motion challenge: cascading.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.680
These complex motions are used
for movements like wagging

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.660
or the curling of a tentacle.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.160
So Mark linked up servos in
a chain, then created a delay

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.450
so each servo moved a little
bit after the one before it,

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.960
causing a chain reaction down the piece.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.590
The more servos, the smoother the motion.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.300
Mark first achieved this effect

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.290
when building Sammael's tentacle
mane in 2004's "Hellboy."

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.132
He realized that the cascading quality

00:04:30.132 --> 00:04:32.710
could be retrofitted to the Scrunt's tail.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.150
When the delay is set to
zero, the tail curls up.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.970
Crank up the delay, and
it wags like a real tail.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.683
The cascade controller
also works on fingers.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.500
By distributing motion
from finger to finger,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.860
Mark created the illusion
of finger drumming

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.350
on King Balor's hand
from 2008's "Hellboy II."

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.920
Despite their amazing range,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.500
radio-controlled servos have their limits.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:57.930
So, one of the things about robotics

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.900
that most people don't know
is that pound for pound,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.560
robots have one-tenth the
strength of humans or animals.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.493
Narrator: As you can see here,

00:05:05.493 --> 00:05:07.635
the Scrunt can look side to side,

00:05:07.635 --> 00:05:09.376
but it can't tilt its head,

00:05:09.376 --> 00:05:12.250
because they couldn't fit the
servos needed in its neck.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.816
Mark: Those servos are great,

00:05:13.816 --> 00:05:17.340
but they're fairly simplistic
in the way they're controlled,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.940
and they just do one thing.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.100
You have to make some decisions

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.750
about what you're going to leave out.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.100
Narrator: But fast-forward
to 2021's "The Tomorrow War."

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.550
This terrifying White Spike animatronic

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.880
that Mark helped create can tilt its head,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.740
thanks to major
technological advancements.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.010
The key here was a modern
digital industrial servo.

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.370
Unlike the large servos
used on the Scrunt,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.680
Mark says these ones are
specifically designed

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.032
to fit inside of a robot without
sacrificing their strength.

00:05:44.032 --> 00:05:45.890
This thing is ridiculously strong.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:49.680
So we can put a big, heavy
head on the end of this thing,

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.540
and it can handle it.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.700
I can command these with high precision,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.180
which has really not been
possible prior to that.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.580
Narrator: This six-axis module

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.440
has six degrees of freedom.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.120
Mark: It can turn, it can
tilt, and it can also shift,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.040
and it can change length,
too, which is very unusual.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.250
Narrator: Like Edward and the Scrunt,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.760
the White Spike is moved by joysticks.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.350
This time, Mark used a
software system he built

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.690
called the Motion Synthesizer.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.410
Mark: The functions are
represented by little boxes

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.940
with patch cords connecting them together.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.220
So by connecting the nodes
together on patch cords,

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.830
it's really easy to
visualize what's happening

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:25.810
from top to bottom.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:27.437
Narrator: While the module's tilt motion

00:06:27.437 --> 00:06:30.800
looks rough at first, once
Mark adds filters to it,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.690
it becomes smoother and
therefore more lifelike.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.073
Take some of that filtering off,

00:06:35.073 --> 00:06:36.990
and it can go much faster.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.224
Mark: As I crank this up to around 60,

00:06:40.224 --> 00:06:43.920
you can see the movement
of the puppet component

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.940
is now becoming very fluid.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.400
Narrator: No matter how much more powerful

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.685
the technology gets, the finished product

00:06:49.685 --> 00:06:52.890
is only as good as the
brains behind the operation.

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:54.370
You can always get the puppet working,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.187
but you have to get it working

00:06:56.187 --> 00:06:57.950
at the exact moment it's
supposed to be working.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.810
Sometimes it doesn't
always work out that way,

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.704
so you got to adapt.

